 other  principles so when did this integration  why this very tight integration between  the two concepts of  of the agronomy and and these particular  social benefits  how did that happen  i i didn't plant him i swear what a  great question  um so uh when did it happen  i think in it uh in part it happened  um with social movements it was from the  start because they were looking for  social benefits from agricultural  production  not just increased production or more  diversified production or not just  environmental benefits so in latin  america for example  uh in brazil and and in mexico and other  countries where there was  um small holder organizing around  trying to ensure uh environmental and  social benefits from agriculture  there was uh from the start this linking  between  ecological production and  making sure that this benefited more  than just  uh a few um  certainly um the scientific development  of agroecology back in the 30s  was largely focused on just the marrying  of  agronomy and ecology and not paying  attention to this  a social dimension but starting i would  say around the 70s  this connection between the kind of  social side  and the political side and the um  agronomic and ecological side became  more tightly aligned and it's been  there's been a lot of scholarship and a  lot of advocacy that these  two are connected if you only have the  agronomic and ecological side then you  might have  environmental benefits to agricultural  production but you may not see any  impact in terms of  food security or health benefits or  livelihoods for small scale producers so  it's crucial that those  two get connected if you want to have a  sort of  multi-faceted  food system that that has benefits in  all of those dimensions  okay thanks uh that's clear i just think  then the title is  uh a bit misleading it seems like there  should be maybe two words or something  because as you say  uh the idea of using ecological  principles within agriculture is fine  and and that is earlier but that's very  clear thanks a lot  great any other questions  yes ma'am i have a question just  expanding on gender equity  i was hoping maybe the classes that the  males is it just the males attend or do  you also have females that attend it  and what what goes on in those courses  maybe some examples  i appreciate it  thanks thanks jeremiah um so  the classes there's there's a range of  different strategies so we've been  trying  a number it it is not easy to address  gender inequity  in the united states let alone in other  countries right so  um this isn't something that's solved  overnight and we have tried over the  last 20  20 years a number of different things  we've tried small group discussions  where we've had all men all women and  actually divided by  age and in small group discussions  facilitated by  community members and um and then coming  together in a large group and talking  about those  uh small group discussions we found that  to have some  impact we've tried these community  events  where it's both men and women and almost  in a  in a kind of celebratory almost a  performance  demonstrating you know different ways of  being together but  um with these recipe days we've  used theater where we've had different  plays which local people  perform and then discuss um  you know different issues that come up  whether it's alcoholism  or domestic violence or um  uh you know unequal division of labor  um uh so that's also been quite  effective  um so and then even peer-to-peer  uh visiting and discussion um has been  a feature so a range of different things  some some  which are sex segregated and some which  are integrated  and uh it it's you know sort of dialogue  based as a as one of the real emphasis  areas that was  that was beautiful thank you ma'am  are there more questions for rachel  if not rachel maybe i can ask you one  andy i've got a question if  sorry go ahead phil um  rachel that was a wonderful presentation  and and i was particularly taken with  your  multiple partnerships um pathways sorry  multiple pathways to food security i  thought that was a terrific  slide and and when you were talking  about that um you talked about how  um there was a change over that time  between  members of uh families working off  their own farm working for other farmers  and then  as conditions improved on the the  individual farms  ecologically and nutritionally there was  less need for  uh folks to go off their own farm and  more need of course  staying on the farm how did the social  networks change  across that period i mean i  i assume what you were getting at is  that with the rising agro-ecological  methods there was more seed and  information sharing um  was that mediated by um your project or  was that  did that sort of spring up um you know  simultaneously automatically and um yeah  what kind of social relations were there  among farmers where  folks were working you know across  different farms  previously right i mean so  previously um the more food insecure  households when they ran out of their  own food stocks would go and work on  other people's farms  sometimes for pay and sometimes just for  food  that's been a long-standing practice  there's some  special name for that um called ganyu  and it's it's um considered um  a social obligation to hire someone if  they are  short of their own food um but it also  is exploitative  um and uh previously i've done research  on that dynamic and it can be  you can imagine you have no food in your  home you're going to a neighbor who's  better off and asking can i work for you  for food it's not  it's not it can be quite humiliating um  there is a lot of seed sharing uh  collectively  um informally and reciprocity is a very  important  social norm in malawi and uh but not so  much information sharing  so i would say certainly our  intervention fostered more  information sharing between farmers we  we have found previously that there's  very little information sharing about  farming  and in fact one study we did found only  10 percent of households  talked about farming even between  husbands and wives uh so  there isn't a tradition of a lot of  information sharing  seed sharing yes um and so i think that  the there was a lot of emphasis in the  intervention around farmers  talking to other farmers and sharing  information um  but then what they also talked about  in those interviews is how they they  went from being kind of a  beggar essentially to being someone who  could be very  generous and that this you know  facilitated more  social networks for them so that was  something that they really  that was something very important to  them that they strongly appreciated  um and increased their social networks  thanks and i think also  just on that point they there's a big  emphasis in our work on  experimentation that farmers do their  own experiments and i think that  was very empowering for farmers to think  about them doing their own experiments  they  try out their own thing and they talked  about how they were  encouraged by the fact that it was a  fellow poor farmers who were also using  these methods  and that it wasn't just the well-off  farmer who was using these methods so  that was important too good  um i have a question sorry i can't turn  my camera on i'm having some  broadband issues but um a little bit of  a departure from mr mcmichael's question  but um kind of building off jeremiah's  i was wondering if you could explain a  little bit more what the  um child nutrition education looked like  and then also what some of your  measurements were for  um increased nutrition and  um because i just have so many questions  about the nutrition related aspect of  things i was wondering if  um given the initial lack of diversity  and diet that you were talking about if  there are any widespread  nutrient deficiencies that are present  in the area that  were worked on uh specifically in  