 it's kind of a  mixture of all those things  thank you so much there are more  questions for rachel  we're a little bit over time rachel  apologies  uh let me ask you just one quick  question for  for future discussion probably um but  the  approach that you've used has been  multi-faceted in terms of agronomic  interventions  and uh including things in crop genetics  and also very training heavy in the  sense of deep engagement with a few  number of farmers and i'm  curious if you could comment on some of  your ideas  around scaling both and maybe a triage  based approach  we all know that certain packages  practices are difficult to extend in the  near term  and that extension staff in eastern  southern africa  usually it's quite limited so i'm  curious based on what you've learned  from this deep engagement  with these farmer groups if you have  some some key insights on  how to accelerate some transformations  beyond the direct areas where you've  worked  yeah it's a big question i think i  i think it has to involve um  farmer to farmer teaching and  experimentation  because it has to be so um  agro ecosystem appropriate so i feel  like there always has to be some  component of  farmers and the social piece so farmers  finding the right  combination for their context um but  i think you know the kind of agroecology  schools that have there have been a  number of them established in latin  america which are led by  farmer organizations or social movement  organizations might be a strategy for  scaling out this approach  but with technical backstopping from  scientists working alongside those  organizations  um i don't have a lot of confidence  in um the extension system  working with this model because the  extension system  as far as i have seen it operate largely  uses this kind of tech  transfer um really you know  uh expert driven model um and sort of  input driven model  um if it were possible to  you know revert you know really overhaul  the extension system to take that  approach  i think that would be exciting i just  i'm not sure that really  would be possible in the way that it's  operated to date so i think i would  i would i would think about these kind  of  schools and then support for  uh farmer-led initiatives  in different places um  i don't know if that fits the bill and  i'm not that's kind of what i have in  mind  one of one of the grand challenges  before us um  thanks a lot rachel that was fantastic  you had a lot to the seminar series this  this semester and we thank you for your  presentation  and your participation in the in the  panel they're both great  thanks so much and thanks a lot for  everyone attending today  and and throughout the semester i hope  everyone has a decent next couple weeks  as we finish things up  be well thanks again bye-bye  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


